From: OdroidLinux SDK
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: rk818: the current's zero from a charge
 balance; the counter across power-offs; sleep and rest; a panic keeps
 the level

What 0035 learnt from was counted with a wrong zero, and a reset of the
SoC alone (a panic) read the level from an OCV sample 7 h old. Measured
on both GO Ultras on 2026-11-21, the first one with a USB tester inline.

The current's zero
- POFFSET was written as a byte and read back unsigned: the second
  unit's -33 came back as +223, and every periodic calibration (8 min)
  put the zero 256 codes (771 mA) off; its log steps between -3 and
  -771 mA with the voltage unchanged. It is read signed now. The first
  unit with its charge inhibited and the board on USB (4 W more load
  moved the reading by 9 mA, the battery idle): about -740 mA read
  before (noted at the time, that log not kept), -15 to -36 mA after.
- The zero found at the end of a charge (current read as 0) hides what
  the battery gives the system then: on a PC port, from one end of a
  charge to the next (a top-up between, the battery back in the same
  state) the second unit's counter took in 95-106 mAh over 26-35 min
  each time, 190 mA. The zero now comes from that balance
  (rk818_balance()): over at least 60 min, between ends seen awake after
  a charge that put in 1% or more and began at the end's charge voltage,
  dropped when POFFSET changes or a sleep falls in it. The end-of-charge
  zero is the first estimate only. With both errors the second unit's
  readings were 771 - 190 = 578 mA too negative (no meter there: its
  2 A charge read 1441 mA, 2019 with that), and the "4.8 Ah" it counted
  from full to its brown-out was 3.06 Ah.
- In s2idle the gauge reads about 210 mA high: on the first unit, the
  battery idle, the counter took in 40 mAh over 11.6 min (driver log:
  3190 -> 3230 mAh) and read 210-219 mA at the wake; off the charger 44
  and 42 mAh over 35 min with the board drawing about 150 mA. Awake it
  reads 15 mA off. At the wake the counter over the sleep is put right
  by that (the same test then: 1776 -> 1778 mAh). Measured on the first
  unit only. The correction of 07c438f (full again after a sleep by the
  voltage) goes: its 3040 mAh in 5 h were the byte bug and this.

The counter across power-offs, and what happened between two runs
- The PMIC's counter runs on through a reboot, a power-off with USB in
  and U-Boot's wait there, U-Boot's power-off when the charger is pulled
  in that wait, and a power-up by the key later; it counts the charge in
  the wait (first unit: 3104 -> 3336 mAh in 10 min at 1.3 A; 3142 ->
  3198 after 3 min, unplugged, 5 min off, the key) and nothing with the
  PMIC off (2922 -> 2922 mAh over 2 min off).
- What happened is read, not guessed (first unit, RK818 dumps at probe
  after a reboot, a panic by sysrq, a power-off and the key, a power-up
  on USB, a power-off on USB and the key):
  - VB_MON's low-voltage field, which this driver keeps at 3.0 V (3.4 V
    asleep), is back at its 3.2 V after any reset of the PMIC (a
    power-up, the DEV_OFF_RST of a reboot and of a power-off on USB) and
    stays after a panic (0x14/0x54 against 0x12). The MFD core leaves it
    to this driver now (0037).
  - The driver writes how it went down in MISC_MARK (a reboot, a
    power-off on USB: the PMIC stays on, no new OCV sample), U-Boot (0018)
    what it did (waited charging; its first wait since: this power-up's
    sample, if new, is from before any charge, kept in OCV_USED when the
    PMIC was reset; powered the PMIC off) and where the counter was when it left the wait (bits 23:16): if
    the counter moved from there by more than a step, something ran after
    the wait (another system) and none of U-Boot's marks stands. Linux
    puts that mark far from the counter at its boot, so that a wait left
    without one (the key held there) matches no old one.
  - A new OCV sample: a PMIC reset after no shutdown (a brown-out, a key
    held) or after a power-off on the battery or by U-Boot. A panic (the
    SoC alone) gives none: before, the boot took the sample of the last
    power-up (3613 mV, a brown-out 7 h before, the battery full on the
    charger) for a brown-out and set the level to 0%.
- A change of the counter since the last save is a charge when U-Boot
  waited (or Linux powered off on USB, or the PMIC came up on USB: an
  older U-Boot waits then too); it is in the learning cycle. Else
  another system ran (its gauge, its draw): the level is the lowest of
  the counter, the saved one and what the battery says (a new OCV, else
  the voltage less the load's drop), and the cycle drops; after a power
  lost (no shutdown) no more than an empty battery and what the counter
  took in since. The charge is saved signed: below 0 the counter runs on
  with a FCC too small.
- The first boot after 0035 (no boot state): its cycle and a FCC above
  the initial one were counted with the zero off: dropped, nothing learnt
  from that boot.
- Another system on the console (an SD card) is out of the scope (the
  user's call); what it changes in the counter is mostly caught as above
  and then safe, not in every order of events.

The OCV at a power-on
- With a charge counted, the sample of this power-up is before it
  (U-Boot's first wait), after it (a power-up by the key), or of no known
  place (between charges, or an older U-Boot): then the level is the
  lower of the counter and the sample plus the charge. After a power lost
  (no shutdown: a brown-out, a key held, a hang; a new sample) the counter
  may be off (a FCC too large, the work not saving in a hang): the new
  sample bounds the level from above (after a load it reads low, after a
  charge high), plus the charge; and U-Boot's sample of its first wait
  (kept when the PMIC was reset) plus the charge, also when U-Boot's
  marks do not stand (the key held in the wait: the counter's change is
  the charge then).
  With an older U-Boot after a brown-out and a charge the level can be
  above the battery's by a FCC's error (no sample from before the
  charge): a known limit until 0018 is flashed.
- It tells the end of a cycle for the learning when no charge came or it
  is before it, and only after 30 min off (LEARN_REST_MIN), from a 0%
  power-off at any OCV (a FCC far too small leaves half at 0%: D26): a power key
  held at 15% and the board on again at once (by the key or the charger)
  reads as low as a brown-out (the voltage not back after the load yet),
  and what is not known is not learnt (the user's rule). A FCC too large
  is then put right by a brown-out with 30 min off before the charger,
  or by the 0% cycles.
- It gives the level after 120 min off (on the board 3741 -> 3770 mV over
  35 min after 1.67 A; the simulator's model leaves some 9 mV after 30
  min, 6-8% in the flat 20-30%), or when the cycle ended at the bottom
  (a brown-out, 0%), plus a charge counted after it; else the counter
  does.
- The OCV table is a fit of the second unit's logged discharge with the
  zero corrected (scripts/battery-fit.py --current-offset-ma 578): the
  ends of 10 min phases at 0.36 A less 165 mOhm times the current, 0% the
  3610 mV at rest after the brown-out. Those points are likely a few mV
  under the OCV at rest the driver reads it with (the fit's 0% came out
  at 3599): not measured yet.
- From a 0% power-off only what the OCV says is left beyond 5% counts
  (ZERO_UNCERT_PCT): the bottom of the table is known to some mV (8 mV off
  made a brown-out every fourth cycle); the FCC comes up from below to
  where 0% leaves about 8%. A brown-out is an OCV at most 10% (3624 mV).

Rest and sleep
- The PMIC's relaxed voltage is no OCV here: it takes it once, at any
  current, and holds it (3934 mV during a 1.58 A charge took the first
  unit from 43% to 78% on the charger). The re-anchor to it goes. After
  60 min asleep off the charger the voltage at the wake less R and the
  polarisation times the sleep's current re-anchors the counter (a game
  before a sleep holds the voltage down for half an hour and more: 3741
  -> 3770 mV in 35 min after 1.67 A; charging at 1.58 A, inhibited: 250
  mV at once, 53 more in 20 min, still falling). Not seen on the board
  yet: its sleeps were 35 min.

The level
- It rises on the charger only, not by the current's sign (it stayed 0
  through a charge with the zero off); towards the limit at the end of a
  charge by 1% a cycle (66 -> 100 at once before).
- "charge finish" once per charge, not every 5 s (1485 lines in 3 h had
  pushed the boot lines out of the log).
- Nothing learnt yet: 75% of the design capacity, so that 0% comes early
  rather than a brown-out above it.

Also
- Hibernation: the boot kernel decides the level, the FCC and the cycle
  at its power-on; the restored one takes them over (a boot generation
  in HALT_CNT tells it another kernel ran). Not tried on the board.
- Counter differences in raw units: nothing rounded off at its sets.
- The tuning constants carry their tested ranges: the matrix passes at
  their edges (scripts/rk818-sim/sweep.py) and fails far outside them
  (mutate.py); just outside it need not fail.
- The sleep's correction only after s2idle (where it was measured), not
  after a hibernation's thaw. The shutdown saves the charge of now, not
  of the last work cycle (5 s before: counted twice at the next boot).
- The third of three s2idle sleeps of the first unit's discharge test
  did not wake (red LED; recovered by the charger and the key); not the
  gauge's, its cause not found.

scripts/rk818-sim: a scenario matrix through probe/work/suspend/resume/
shutdown over a model of the PMIC, the cell (with its polarisation),
the charger and U-Boot's wait, 115 cells; each fix disabled in turn fails
at least one, and so does each rule of U-Boot 0018 in the model
(scripts/rk818-sim/mutate.py).
